  "why": "Both runs already cover all 102 passages (P01-P102, no gaps, no duplicates, no empty subtopic), so the disagreement is about where boundaries fall. The decisive evidence is the heading numbering in the evidence, which is sequential: passages under a sub-heading must come after all passages of its parent section. Run B breaks that in four subtopics -- it assigns P19, P20 to 4.2.1 Law of Dominance and P21 to 4.2.2 Law of Segregation while sending P22-P25 back to 4.2, and pulls P34 forward into 4.2.2.1 -- so its 4.2 / 4.2.1 / 4.2.2 blocks interleave, which a linearly numbered chapter cannot do. Run A keeps strict document order everywhere except the P42-P43 placement with the dominance explanation, which both runs independently agree on, so that one is treated as a real layout interleave rather than an error. I therefore took run A's partition as the base. The one thing run B adds that run A's own subtopic name concedes ('... and the test cross') is that the test cross is a separate NEET-testable block beginning at P26; B pins it at P26-P29 and that boundary sits inside run A's contiguous P21-P29 block without contradicting A's ordering, so I split A's subtopic 4 into 'Punnett square and monohybrid phenotypic and genotypic ratios' (P21-P25) and 'Test cross' (P26-P29). I also took B's name for the 4.8.1 block ('Genetic disorders and pedigree analysis') since both runs give it identical passages and that name also accounts for the 4.8 introductory text; that is a naming choice only, no passage moved. Verified on the written list: 23 subtopics, the union of their passages is exactly P01-P102 with every passage covered once, and no subtopic has an empty passage list. Note that the evidence for this adjudication contained only the heading list, not the passage texts, so the boundary calls rest on heading order and on agreement between the two runs rather than on reading the passages; hence medium confidence.",
